{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rally (positive slope), the model lags and sits ~5–12 EUR below realized levels across most of the 30D horizon. Apply an upward level shift that increases with recent momentum (bigger shift when the 10D change is large).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: the negative bias is smallest in the first few days and grows into the mid-horizon (≈day 7–20). Use a ramp correction (small add-on days 1–3, larger add-on days 7–20, then taper).",
    "Post-spike mean-reversion overshoot: after a sharp jump in history (local peak in last 1–3 days), the model often projects too much continuation and misses the subsequent pullback (true outcomes soften later). Damp forecast changes after recent spikes by pulling days 5–30 back toward a medium-term mean (e.g., 20–30D average).",
    "Volatility compression: forecasts are too smooth versus realized moves (miss both the magnitude of rallies and the depth of later retracements). Increase responsiveness by scaling forecast deviations from the recent mean (i.e., widen the amplitude) but combine with spike-damping to avoid runaway continuation.",
    "Turning-point lag: when the market transitions from rally to decline (peak then drift down), the model remains too high for too long. Introduce a regime switch: if last price < 3–5D peak by a threshold, apply a downward tilt to the far horizon (days 10–30)."
  ]
}
